        {
            "id": "110924031040738390",
            "user_id": "109362677722509349",
            "content": "<p>We live in a highly bushfire-prone area in Australia. These are our annual house insurance premiums in the past six years:<br>2018 - $2645<br>2019 - $2745<br>2020 - $3124<br>2021 - $3396<br>2022 - $4069<br>2023 - $5582<br>If <a href=\"https://aus.social/tags/insurance\" class=\"mention hashtag\" rel=\"nofollow noopener\" target=\"_blank\">#<span>insurance</span></a> becomes unaffordable unless you live in a low-risk area, a significant percentage of Australians will be unable to insure their homes because of <a href=\"https://aus.social/tags/bushfire\" class=\"mention hashtag\" rel=\"nofollow noopener\" target=\"_blank\">#<span>bushfire</span></a>, <a href=\"https://aus.social/tags/flood\" class=\"mention hashtag\" rel=\"nofollow noopener\" target=\"_blank\">#<span>flood</span></a>, <a href=\"https://aus.social/tags/cyclone\" class=\"mention hashtag\" rel=\"nofollow noopener\" target=\"_blank\">#<span>cyclone</span></a> risk.<br>So what does that mean in a future where these <a href=\"https://aus.social/tags/climate\" class=\"mention hashtag\" rel=\"nofollow noopener\" target=\"_blank\">#<span>climate</span></a> events are more likely and more severe? <br>If the insurance industry decides a significant percentage of Australians are uninsurable, and that same percentage are highly like to experience extreme weather events, whose responsibility is to to support those individuals when the inevitable happens?<br>Sure, we could argue that people shouldn't live in fire/flood prone areas, but when those areas include entire towns eg Lismore, or suburbs of major cities eg Canberra, that argument doesn't work. We can't all live in CBD high-rises. So where are we going to live in a <a href=\"https://aus.social/tags/climatechange\" class=\"mention hashtag\" rel=\"nofollow noopener\" target=\"_blank\">#<span>climatechange</span></a> future?<br>I don't have answers here. I'm assuming that our home will burn in my lifetime. I'm privileged that - for the moment - we can afford these premiums. But this price rises are going to create an uninsured underclass, who will be already disadvantaged economically, and who will bear the brunt of climate-change impacts.</p>"
        },
        {
            "id": "110924224627428595",
            "user_id": "109363397035999466",
            "content": "<p><span class=\"h-card\" translate=\"no\"><a href=\"https://aus.social/@biancanogrady\" class=\"u-url mention\" rel=\"nofollow noopener\" target=\"_blank\">@<span>biancanogrady</span></a></span> Time for governments local/state/federal who permitted the zoning of land for residential development in flood/fire zones to step up to the plate and assist with resettlement</p>"
        },
        {
            "id": "110924512879433313",
            "user_id": "109375847846569437",
            "content": "<p><span class=\"h-card\" translate=\"no\"><a href=\"https://mastodon.social/@AndrewBurgessPhD\" class=\"u-url mention\" rel=\"nofollow noopener\" target=\"_blank\">@<span>AndrewBurgessPhD</span></a></span> <span class=\"h-card\" translate=\"no\"><a href=\"https://aus.social/@biancanogrady\" class=\"u-url mention\" rel=\"nofollow noopener\" target=\"_blank\">@<span>biancanogrady</span></a></span> <br>Of course when many developments took place at a time when the increased risks were probably not present. </p><p>Global warming is too expensive for insurance. We're rapidly moving to the point where insurance won't be profitable right across the planet.</p><p>Lots of unintended consequences of a hot, wet world.</p>"
        },
        {
            "id": "110924543717148827",
            "user_id": "109362677722509349",
            "content": "<p><span class=\"h-card\"><a href=\"https://mastodon.social/@AndrewBurgessPhD\" class=\"u-url mention\" rel=\"nofollow noopener\" target=\"_blank\">@<span>AndrewBurgessPhD</span></a></span> Ideally yes, but then we come up against the construction/development industry, which has so much power in Australia that I can't see local govts having much chance of preventing development eg flood-prone land in western Sydney is a prime example.</p>"
        },
        {
            "id": "110924594087852997",
            "user_id": "109363397035999466",
            "content": "<p><span class=\"h-card\" translate=\"no\"><a href=\"https://aus.social/@biancanogrady\" class=\"u-url mention\" rel=\"nofollow noopener\" target=\"_blank\">@<span>biancanogrady</span></a></span> Fair point, but I can\u2019t see developers ever paying a cent. I can see us taxpayers all paying for rich seafront suburbs to get very expensive sea walls though. Be nice if they pitch in to help pay for others to relocate.</p>"
        },
        {
            "id": "110927021533047395",
            "user_id": "109594404608507286",
            "content": "<p><span class=\"h-card\"><a href=\"https://aus.social/@biancanogrady\" class=\"u-url mention\" rel=\"nofollow noopener\" target=\"_blank\">@<span>biancanogrady</span></a></span> Queensland has been grappling with this problem for years. It\u2019s easier to build resilience for floods and cyclones than fires though. </p><p><a href=\"https://aus.social/tags/auspol\" class=\"mention hashtag\" rel=\"nofollow noopener\" target=\"_blank\">#<span>auspol</span></a> <a href=\"https://aus.social/tags/climateChange\" class=\"mention hashtag\" rel=\"nofollow noopener\" target=\"_blank\">#<span>climateChange</span></a> </p><p><a href=\"https://insurancecouncil.com.au/resource/insurance-council-welcomes-queensland-funding-boost/\" rel=\"nofollow noopener\" target=\"_blank\"><span class=\"invisible\">https://</span><span class=\"ellipsis\">insurancecouncil.com.au/resour</span><span class=\"invisible\">ce/insurance-council-welcomes-queensland-funding-boost/</span></a></p>"
        },
        {
            "id": "110929405571282880",
            "user_id": "109369531151525039",
            "content": "<p><span class=\"h-card\" translate=\"no\"><a href=\"https://aus.social/@biancanogrady\" class=\"u-url mention\" rel=\"nofollow noopener\" target=\"_blank\">@<span>biancanogrady</span></a></span> You have encapsulated my thoughts exactly, but when I have tried to raise my fears, rather inarticulately, the pushback is intense\u2026</p>"
        },
        {
            "id": "110929631553534344",
            "user_id": "109507554161635993",
            "content": "<p><span class=\"h-card\"><a href=\"https://aus.social/@biancanogrady\" class=\"u-url mention\" rel=\"nofollow noopener\" target=\"_blank\">@<span>biancanogrady</span></a></span> some highly vulnerable communities address this problem through building code changes and through discounts for homeowners who take steps to mitigate their risk, such as cutting down vegetation near the structure, fire (or in high wind areas) high wind resistant roofs and windows, and other strategies. That's probably your best hope. It's not fair to make the people who live in low risk areas or the central business district subsidize other's choices to live in high risk areas.</p>"
        },
        {
            "id": "110929679261259040",
            "user_id": "109362677722509349",
            "content": "<p><span class=\"h-card\"><a href=\"https://urbanists.social/@Ed_Carley\" class=\"u-url mention\" rel=\"nofollow noopener\" target=\"_blank\">@<span>Ed_Carley</span></a></span> Totally agree - I'd love to see the insurance industry adjust premiums based on a home's preparedness, but that would require them to do annual inspections, and can't see them putting the effort into that if it means they get less money!<br>Also, have to point out that a lot of people don't choose to live in high-risk areas: these areas also tend to be lower income because the land/property prices are cheaper eg floodplains, urban fringe. Also, farming communities are often in high-risk areas because that's where the agriculture is.</p>"
        },
        {
            "id": "110929814554428479",
            "user_id": "109507554161635993",
            "content": "<p><span class=\"h-card\"><a href=\"https://aus.social/@biancanogrady\" class=\"u-url mention\" rel=\"nofollow noopener\" target=\"_blank\">@<span>biancanogrady</span></a></span> for the major investments, such as fire resistant roofs, that's a one time improvement (ok, once every 25 years), and only requires a single inspection. Vegetation is tougher, but solutions like the one that contributed to saving this house - <a href=\"https://www.civilbeat.org/2023/08/what-saved-the-miracle-house-in-lahaina/\" rel=\"nofollow noopener\" target=\"_blank\"><span class=\"invisible\">https://www.</span><span class=\"ellipsis\">civilbeat.org/2023/08/what-sav</span><span class=\"invisible\">ed-the-miracle-house-in-lahaina/</span></a> - are also durable enough to not require repeat inspections. I'm all for changing zoning so more people can live close in, but the idea of banning single family zoning isn't popular.</p>"
        },
        {
            "id": "110929822909855755",
            "user_id": "109507554161635993",
            "content": "<p><span class=\"h-card\" translate=\"no\"><a href=\"https://aus.social/@biancanogrady\" class=\"u-url mention\" rel=\"nofollow noopener\" target=\"_blank\">@<span>biancanogrady</span></a></span> people in agricultural communities can take steps to protect their buildings too. It might take action from your state/provincial or federal govt (sorry, in the U.S. here) to make insurers offer discounts for adding mitigation measures, but in the end, everyone wins. Fewer peoples' homes burn, the insurance companies pay out less in disasters, which means they make more, even with lower premiums. Remember they also pay premiums to re-insurers.</p>"
        },
        {
            "id": "110930387979578111",
            "user_id": "109369768826925692",
            "content": "<p><span class=\"h-card\"><a href=\"https://aus.social/@biancanogrady\" class=\"u-url mention\" rel=\"nofollow noopener\" target=\"_blank\">@<span>biancanogrady</span></a></span> managed migration seems to be the only way forward <a href=\"https://uk.bookshop.org/p/books/nomad-century-how-to-survive-the-climate-upheaval-gaia-vince/6608939\" rel=\"nofollow noopener\" target=\"_blank\"><span class=\"invisible\">https://</span><span class=\"ellipsis\">uk.bookshop.org/p/books/nomad-</span><span class=\"invisible\">century-how-to-survive-the-climate-upheaval-gaia-vince/6608939</span></a></p>"
        },
        {
            "id": "110936143568518349",
            "user_id": "109340122151377666",
            "content": "<p><span class=\"h-card\" translate=\"no\"><a href=\"https://aus.social/@biancanogrady\" class=\"u-url mention\" rel=\"nofollow noopener\" target=\"_blank\">@<span>biancanogrady</span></a></span> I don\u2019t know how it is in Australia, but here in the U.S. I\u2019m pretty sure no one with enough resources to make a difference will help the people caught in that trap.  Unless we have sudden fundamental political change, people with homes in vulnerable areas will lose everything.</p>"
        },
        {
            "id": "112673115429045739",
            "user_id": "109353414038696589",
            "content": "<p>I am the happy rescuer of a tiny baby swift which I found on the pavement. Three cheers for the volunteers of the Swift and Swallow SOS Facebook group who swung into action to get him to a skilled carer. Apart from a bit of dehydration he is apparently in good condition and likely to make it to Africa. \u2764\ufe0f<br><a href=\"https://mastodon.social/tags/Swift\" class=\"mention hashtag\" rel=\"nofollow noopener\" target=\"_blank\">#<span>Swift</span></a> <a href=\"https://mastodon.social/tags/Wildlife\" class=\"mention hashtag\" rel=\"nofollow noopener\" target=\"_blank\">#<span>Wildlife</span></a> <a href=\"https://mastodon.social/tags/WildlifeRescue\" class=\"mention hashtag\" rel=\"nofollow noopener\" target=\"_blank\">#<span>WildlifeRescue</span></a></p>"
        },
        {
            "id": "112674917510581539",
            "user_id": "111509672586812221",
            "content": "<p><span class=\"h-card\" translate=\"no\"><a href=\"https://mastodon.social/@ProfLouiseL\" class=\"u-url mention\" rel=\"nofollow noopener\" target=\"_blank\">@<span>ProfLouiseL</span></a></span> you should name it Taylor</p>"
        },
        {
            "id": "112676492554061496",
            "user_id": "109467246192212576",
            "content": "<p><span class=\"h-card\" translate=\"no\"><a href=\"https://mastodon.social/@ProfLouiseL\" class=\"u-url mention\" rel=\"nofollow noopener\" target=\"_blank\">@<span>ProfLouiseL</span></a></span> Thank you for posting this. There are swifts in Leith and I have always worried about what to do in this situation.</p>"
        },
        {
            "id": "112676582962698345",
            "user_id": "109353414038696589",
            "content": "<p><span class=\"h-card\" translate=\"no\"><a href=\"https://mastodon.scot/@barfilfarm\" class=\"u-url mention\" rel=\"nofollow noopener\" target=\"_blank\">@<span>barfilfarm</span></a></span> They were fantastic.  I don't know if they have rescuers in Scotland but you could always join the FB group and ask. I've been following them for a couple of years just out of interest, because I love swifts. I never thought I'd actually need to seek their help!</p>"
        },
        {
            "id": "112677455607998717",
            "user_id": "109375376642507089",
            "content": "<p><span class=\"h-card\" translate=\"no\"><a href=\"https://mastodon.scot/@barfilfarm\" class=\"u-url mention\" rel=\"nofollow noopener\" target=\"_blank\">@<span>barfilfarm</span></a></span> there is a pigeon rescuer in Leith that might be able to help you out, and Baby Beaks in Fife. There's a chap across in Dalry (I think) that  holds pigeons for BB. Sspca also, are good with babies (actually they often hand them over to BB).</p><p>When finding a baby animal the first thing to do is get it warm. A lot of folk with the appropriate anatomy pop them down their bras! Hair dryers on low are good. Once warm you have time to find a rehabber. FB groups are great for this.</p>"
        },
        {
            "id": "112677790642242281",
            "user_id": "109467246192212576",
            "content": "<p><span class=\"h-card\" translate=\"no\"><a href=\"https://mastodon.social/@Aerliss\" class=\"u-url mention\" rel=\"nofollow noopener\" target=\"_blank\">@<span>Aerliss</span></a></span> thank you</p>"
        },
        {
            "id": "112678466145281334",
            "user_id": "109353414038696589",
            "content": "<p><span class=\"h-card\" translate=\"no\"><a href=\"https://mastodon.scot/@barfilfarm\" class=\"u-url mention\" rel=\"nofollow noopener\" target=\"_blank\">@<span>barfilfarm</span></a></span> <span class=\"h-card\" translate=\"no\"><a href=\"https://mastodon.social/@Aerliss\" class=\"u-url mention\" rel=\"nofollow noopener\" target=\"_blank\">@<span>Aerliss</span></a></span> In fact I was advised to keep him cool. It was blazing hot and apparently trying to get cool is one reason they fall out of their nests. So I put him in a small cardboard box with airholes in the shed till a transfer could be arranged. (Small so he couldn't flap around too vigorously and hurt himself). Swifts eat only insects so many wildlife rescue places aren't set up to care for them. But your other local bird rescuers can probably advise how to find a swift specialist</p>"
        },
        {
            "id": "112678531961769377",
            "user_id": "109375376642507089",
            "content": "<p><span class=\"h-card\" translate=\"no\"><a href=\"https://mastodon.social/@ProfLouiseL\" class=\"u-url mention\" rel=\"nofollow noopener\" target=\"_blank\">@<span>ProfLouiseL</span></a></span> <span class=\"h-card\" translate=\"no\"><a href=\"https://mastodon.scot/@barfilfarm\" class=\"u-url mention\" rel=\"nofollow noopener\" target=\"_blank\">@<span>barfilfarm</span></a></span> good to know about the cooling, as I've always been told to warm them up, but of course it's rarely been this warm in Scotland when animals are breeding until now.</p><p>Best of luck to your little rescue, both reaching adulthood and freedom, and making that long journey. Such amazing creatures.</p>"
        },
        {
            "id": "112678541874611071",
            "user_id": "109353414038696589",
            "content": "<p><span class=\"h-card\" translate=\"no\"><a href=\"https://mastodon.social/@Aerliss\" class=\"u-url mention\" rel=\"nofollow noopener\" target=\"_blank\">@<span>Aerliss</span></a></span> <span class=\"h-card\" translate=\"no\"><a href=\"https://mastodon.scot/@barfilfarm\" class=\"u-url mention\" rel=\"nofollow noopener\" target=\"_blank\">@<span>barfilfarm</span></a></span> Yes, this hot weather can be really bad for birds. I read about loads of swifts dying in Spain and bats in India, poor little things. Here's hoping my little swift takes to the skies!</p>"
        },
        {
            "id": "112676644908011862",
            "user_id": "109506060246212214",
            "content": "<p><span class=\"h-card\" translate=\"no\"><a href=\"https://mastodon.social/@ProfLouiseL\" class=\"u-url mention\" rel=\"nofollow noopener\" target=\"_blank\">@<span>ProfLouiseL</span></a></span> Wonderful!</p>"
        },
        {
            "id": "112677044274521231",
            "user_id": "109840202335037250",
            "content": "<p><span class=\"h-card\"><a href=\"https://mastodon.social/@ProfLouiseL\" class=\"u-url mention\" rel=\"nofollow noopener\" target=\"_blank\">@<span>ProfLouiseL</span></a></span> what a little fluffbal!</p><p>(I'm in South Africa, always love seeing the swifts!)</p>"
        },
        {
            "id": "112677050596266155",
            "user_id": "109376332273692659",
            "content": "<p><span class=\"h-card\" translate=\"no\"><a href=\"https://mastodon.social/@ProfLouiseL\" class=\"u-url mention\" rel=\"nofollow noopener\" target=\"_blank\">@<span>ProfLouiseL</span></a></span> well done you. The decline in the number of swifts is, for me, one of the starkest signs of our ecological crisis.</p>"
        },
        {
            "id": "112476497711146302",
            "user_id": "109328206758567967",
